1Q;
A Linux server has a total storage capacity of 50 GB. The system must store rapidly growing system logs, user files, and application data. If log files continuously grow, they may consume the entire disk and cause critical services to stop. Therefore, the storage must be divided efficiently and log growth must be controlled.
Constraint Analysis
The important constraints are:
Total storage = 50 GB
Log files must not exceed 10 GB
System files must remain protected from user/application data.
Critical services must continue running.
Disk space must be monitored regularly.
Log rotation must be configured.
Proposed File System Layout
Partition	Size	Purpose
/	10 GB	Operating system and applications
/var	10 GB	Logs, cache and variable data
/home	15 GB	User files
/opt	10 GB	Application data
Swap	2 GB	Memory support
Free/Reserved	3 GB	Emergency disk space
Total	50 GB	
The /var partition is separated from / because system logs are normally stored under /var/log. This prevents rapidly growing logs from filling the root partition.
Log Rotation
logrotate can be used to control log size.
Example configuration:
sudo nano /etc/logrotate.d/mylogs
Configuration:
/var/log/*.log {
    daily
    rotate 7
    compress
    missingok
    notifempty
    size 100M
}
This rotates logs regularly, compresses old logs, and prevents unnecessary disk usage.
Disk Monitoring
df -h
du -sh /var/log/*
A simple monitoring command:
df -h /var | awk 'NR==2 {print $5}'
If the usage becomes high, old logs can be compressed or removed according to the retention policy.
Solution
The recommended layout separates operating system files, logs, user data, and application data. /var is limited to 10 GB, satisfying the requirement that logs must not exceed 10 GB.
A reserved 3 GB space is also maintained for emergencies. Log rotation, compression, and disk monitoring prevent storage exhaustion.
Justification
This design improves system stability because log growth cannot consume the complete root filesystem. Separating partitions provides better fault isolation, while logrotate automatically controls log growth. Reserved free space ensures that critical services have sufficient storage for temporary and emergency operations.
2Q;
The Linux system experiences frequent context switching because of heavy CPU workloads. Context switching introduces overhead because the CPU must save the current process state and load another process state. Real-time processing requires predictable response time.
Hardware upgrades are not possible, so kernel-level optimization must be used.
Constraint Analysis
The major constraints are:
Hardware cannot be upgraded.
CPU workload is high.
Context switching must be reduced.
Real-time processing must be supported.
Critical processes must receive CPU time quickly.
Scheduler Tuning
Linux provides scheduler-related tools and parameters.
CPU scheduling information can be viewed using:
top
or:
htop
CPU affinity can be used to assign a process to specific CPU cores:
taskset -cp 0,1 PID
Process priority can be changed using:
renice -n -5 -p PID
For real-time tasks:
chrt -f 50 ./realtime_program
A real-time scheduling policy can reduce scheduling delays for important tasks.
Kernel Parameters
System performane can be checked using:
sysctl -a | grep sched
Kernel modules can also be managed without rebooting:
lsmod
Load a required module:
sudo modprobe module_name
Remove an unused module:
sudo modprobe -r module_name
Optimization Strategy
The recommended approach is:
Identify CPU-intensive processes.
Assign important real-time processes appropriate CPU affinity.
Use suitable scheduling priorities.
Reduce unnecessary background processes.
Load only required kernel modules.
Monitor context switching and CPU usage.
Avoid unnecessary scheduler overhead.
Justification
Scheduler tuning improves CPU utilization by ensuring that important real-time processes receive CPU time quickly. CPU affinity can reduce unnecessary movement of processes between CPU cores, which can reduce scheduling and cache overhead.
Kernel modules allow required functionality to be added without rebuilding the complete kernel. Together, these techniques improve responsiveness without requiring hardware upgrades.
3Q;
The administrator must process 1 million log entries per day using only shell scripting. The script must complete within 2 minutes.
Processing each line individually with expensive commands can be slow. Therefore, optimized Linux commands should process large amounts of data in batches.
Constraint Analysis
The requirements are:
Input = 1 million log entries
Only shell scripting is allowed.
Maximum execution time = 2 minutes
Processing should use efficient Linux commands.
Unnecessary process creation must be avoided.
Sample Input
Example log file:
INFO user1 login
ERROR database failed
INFO user2 login
WARNING memory high
ERROR connection failed
INFO user3 logout
For a large file:
LOGFILE="server.log"
Optimized Shell Script
#!/bin/bash
LOGFILE="server.log"
echo "Processing log file..."
START=$(date +%s)
echo "Total lines:"
wc -l < "$LOGFILE"
echo "Error count:"
grep -c "ERROR" "$LOGFILE"
echo "Warning count:"
grep -c "WARNING" "$LOGFILE"
echo "Top error messages:"
grep "ERROR" "$LOGFILE" | sort | uniq -c | sort -nr | head -10
END=$(date +%s)
echo "Processing time: $((END-START)) seconds"
Performance Optimization
Instead of using:
while read line
do
done < server.log
for every log entry, optimized commands such as grep, awk, sort, uniq, and wc process data efficiently.
For example:
awk '/ERROR/ {count++} END {print count}' server.log
Multiple statistics can also be calculated in a single awk pass:
awk '
/ERROR/ {error++}
/WARNING/ {warning++}
/INFO/ {info++}
END {
    print "ERROR:", error
    print "WARNING:", warning
    print "INFO:", info
}' server.log
Why These Commands?
Command	Purpose
wc	Counts lines quickly
grep	Searches matching log entries
awk	Performs fast text processing
sort	Sorts large datasets
uniq	Removes/counts duplicate entries
head	Limits output
gzip	Compresses old logs
Justification
The main performance improvement comes from avoiding a shell loop that starts additional commands for every log line. Standard Linux utilities are optimized for bulk text processing and can process large files efficiently.
Using a single awk pass for multiple statistics further reduces unnecessary file reading. Therefore, this approach is suitable for processing approximately 1 million log entries while targeting the required 2-minute limit, subject to the server's CPU, disk speed, and log format.
4Q;
A Linux server contains multiple background services. One faulty process is consuming approximately 90% CPU. The server cannot be restarted because critical services must remain available.
The faulty process must therefore be identified, monitored, and controlled without affecting important services.
Constraint Analysis
The important constraints are:
CPU usage is approximately 90%.
System restart is not allowed.
Critical services must continue running.
The faulty process must be controlled safely.
The process should be monitored before taking action.
Identify the Process
Use
top
or:
ps aux --sort=-%cpu | head
Example output:
USER       PID  %CPU  COMMAND
user      2450  90.0  faulty_app
root       800   5.0  system_service
The PID of the faulty process is:
2450
Monitor the Process
top -p 2450
or:
ps -p 2450 -o pid,ppid,stat,%cpu,%mem,cmd
Reduce CPU Priority
If the process is not immediately dangerous:
renice 10 -p 2450
This gives the process lower CPU scheduling priority.
CPU affinity can also be restricted:
taskset -cp 2 2450
This limits the process to a particular CPU core.
Stop the Process Safely
First attempt:
kill 2450
Check whether it stopped:
ps -p 2450
If it does not terminate, a stronger signal can be used:
kill -9 2450
kill -9 should be used only as a last resort because it does not allow the application to perform cleanup.
Service-Based Process
If the faulty process belongs to a systemd service:
systemctl status service_name
Then:
sudo systemctl restart service_name
or, if necessary:
sudo systemctl stop service_name
Justification
The process should first be identified and monitored before taking action. Lowering its priority or restricting CPU affinity can reduce its impact while maintaining service availability.
If the process is confirmed to be faulty, terminating only that process avoids rebooting the complete server. This approach protects critical services and restores CPU availability.
5Q;
A physical server has 16 GB RAM and must host 5 virtual machines. Each VM requires at least 2 GB, while the host operating system requires 4 GB.
Constraint Analysis
Required RAM:
5 VMs × 2 GB = 10 GB
Host requirement:
4 GB
Total minimum:
10 GB + 4 GB = 14 GB
Available:
16 GB
Remaining:
16 GB - 14 GB = 2 GB
Therefore, the requirements can be satisfied.
Basic Allocation
Component	RAM
Host OS	4 GB
VM1	2 GB
VM2	2 GB
VM3	2 GB
VM4	2 GB
VM5	2 GB
Reserved/Buffer	2 GB
Total	16 GB
Recommended Strategy
The safest allocation is:
Host OS = 4 GB
VM1 = 2 GB
VM2 = 2 GB
VM3 = 2 GB
VM4 = 2 GB
VM5 = 2 GB
Reserved = 2 GB
The 2 GB reserve should not be permanently assigned to the VMs. It can provide additional flexibility for host operations and temporary workload increases.
Virtualization Concepts
1. Memory Reservation
Each VM should have a minimum reservation of 2 GB so that it can operate reliably.
2. Memory Ballooning
Memory ballooning can allow the hypervisor to reclaim unused memory from VMs when the host needs additional RAM.
3. Dynamic Memory
If supported by the virtualization platform, VM memory can be dynamically adjusted based on workload.
4. Monitoring
Memory usage should be continuously monitored:
free -h
For virtualization environments, the administrator can also monitor VM memory through the hypervisor management tools.
Justification
The minimum VM requirement is:
5 × 2 GB = 10 GB
Adding the host requirement:
10 GB + 4 GB = 14 GB
Since the server has 16 GB, all five VMs can run with their minimum requirement while leaving 2 GB available as a safety buffer.
This allocation satisfies all constraints and prevents the host from becoming memory-starved. Dynamic memory and ballooning can further improve resource utilization when workloads change.
